    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2279b6da_0de5fb0e",
        "filename": "designs/redfish-graphql-server.md",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 297,
      "author": {
        "id": 1001712
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-30T10:15:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The argument about `clear architectural boundary` does not apply. We already have very clear architectural boundaries e.g. in `dbus-sensors` where folks maintain separate applications with minimal code sharing.\n\nEven in `entity-manager` you have multiple applications even using different sdbusplus bindings (callback vs async API).\n\nThere is no limitation to the amount of separation you can have in one repository. You can even copy-paste utility functions into your own folder if you do not want to share a single line of code.\n\n\u003e and avoids increasing the complexity and review burden of the bmcweb codebase.\n\nI don\u0027t think you fully understand the implications of maintaining so many different repositories. It actually tends to increase the review burden because folks tend to duplicate utility code and helper functions and then you need to create runtime interfaces between all these services, create multiple gerrit changes in a topic, and synchronize recipe bumps.\n\n\nexample to understand:\n\nif i want to add a parameter to a dbus-sensor, i need:\n  - an implementation patch in `dbus-sensors`\n  - a configuration and schema change in `entity-manager`\n\nand these changes need to go in in the right order. For anything using PDI, that is 3 changes.\n\nIf `dbus-sensors` applications were part of `entity-manager` repo, that would drop down to 2 changes, reducing the review burden.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 295,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 297,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "d288a7b4b79d2dc3b7811a39d885dd1f984d0da6",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2fe1699e_5f230e7c",
        "filename": "designs/redfish-graphql-server.md",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 297,
      "author": {
        "id": 1001762
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-05T05:51:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t have any objection to keeping the GraphQL server code in a separate folder within bmcweb. My concern is more around downstream consumption. Since bmcweb and the GraphQL server may evolve independently and follow different development priorities, downstream projects that need to track the latest changes from only one of these components may still be forced to rebase onto updates from both. This could become a significant burden, especially if one component diverges substantially from upstream over time.\n\nIs there a good solution to address this challenge?",
